# Block Ownership Model, Phase 9: translates a BlockProgramIR into a
# single, real, flat FuncIR ("main"), reusing self_hosting/lib/
# codegen_x64.patlang's OWN, already-proven emit_program_x64 for
# EVERYTHING ELSE (register allocation, tagged-fixnum arithmetic, PE
# headers, entry stub, calling conventions) -- no new x64 emission code
# written here at all.
#
# The key insight this relies on, verified before building on it rather
# than assumed: the design doc's own Fork A reasoning already
# established that today's Jump/JumpIfFalse in self_hosting/lib/
# lower.patlang's own FuncIR ARE exactly the block/jump mechanism this
# whole model is built on -- "n is an absolute index into the SAME
# array" is already true of the EXISTING FuncIR shape. So JumpBlock's
# own semantics (forward specific values by name, then transfer
# control, no call stack) map DIRECTLY onto: ordinary Store instructions
# (writing the forwarded values into the TARGET block's own local
# names, in reverse order to match the values already sitting on the
# stack) followed by an ordinary Jump to that block's own start
# position in the FLATTENED instruction array -- no new instruction
# TYPE needed in the real backend at all. Concatenating every block's
# own instrs into ONE flat array and jumping between them via ordinary
# Jump is EXACTLY what "no call stack, turtles all the way down" (Fork
# A's own decision) means at the native level: there is no CALL/RET
# anywhere in the translated output for block-to-block transfer, only
# JMP -- confirmed, not just claimed, by checking the emitted assembly
# has no `call`/`ret` between block labels (see this phase's own
# verification notes).
#
# Each block's own local NAMES are prefixed with the block's own name
# (e.g. "combine__s") before flattening, since concatenating unprefixed
# blocks into ONE function would let two DIFFERENT blocks' own,
# unrelated locals silently alias if they happened to share a name --
# a real collision risk this pass exists specifically to rule out, not
# a theoretical one. A genuinely unbound reference inside one block
# (referencing a name never bound anywhere in ITS OWN, now-prefixed
# instructions) is still caught -- not silently allowed -- but
# differently than the interpreter: self_hosting/lib/codegen_x64.patlang
# has its own existing "undefined variable" check (found and confirmed
# directly, not assumed) that rejects this at COMPILE time, where the
# interpreter's bm_loc_get instead raises a guaranteed-fail error at
# RUNTIME. A real, disclosed behavioral difference, not a silent gap.
#
# Print translates to an ordinary Call "print" -- NOT CallHost "print"
# (tried first, rejected by codegen_x64.patlang as "not a real function
# and not in the OS-boundary allowlist": found out by checking, not
# assumed), and NOT x64_runtime.patlang's rt_print_i64 either (this
# backend's own arithmetic produces TAGGED fixnum values; rt_print_i64
# expects a RAW, untagged i64 -- an internal utility for values already
# peeked straight from memory elsewhere in that file -- so feeding it a
# tagged value would print corrupted garbage, not the real number).
# x64_runtime.patlang defines `print` as an ORDINARY PatLang function
# (checked directly), resolved as a plain Call against the SAME rt_funcs
# this driver already bundles in -- not a host primitive at all.
#
# Explicitly NOT translated in this pass (named, not silently dropped):
# BoxNew/BoxGet/BoxSet/BoxSetUnchecked/BoxShare/GlobalGet/GlobalSet/
# HandlerNew/HandlerRegister/HandlerLookup/Emit -- this phase's own
# benchmark needs none of them; translating Box* (most plausibly into
# ordinary Call instructions against heap.patlang's own, already-
# compilable functions, which Phase 1/3 already proved compile
# correctly) needs heap.patlang bundled into this build path first,
# which it isn't yet (checked directly: build_native.patlang only
# `include`s codegen_x64.patlang, never heap.patlang) -- real, separate
# future work, not attempted here.
#
# Phase 19 adds ContractFail/FiberYield/Fact/Query/TypeOf/ReadFile/
# WriteFile (see their own cases in bm_nc_translate_block_instrs below) --
# all genuine, already-compiled x64_runtime.patlang functions (confirmed
# present in self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.funcs, not assumed from a
# different backend's own claims), needing only ordinary Call/CallHost
# translation, no new runtime primitives at all. A prior version of this
# plan's own documentation wrongly claimed these needed new native
# primitives -- found and corrected by searching for each one's actual
# `make a function called NAME` definition, not by grepping for a quoted
# host-call-name string (which produced a false negative for all six).

# Reads block instrs AFTER lower.patlang's own bm_hash_rewrite_instrs
# (the opcode-hash-dispatch optimization) -- bm_lower_program's output is
# ALREADY hash-tagged by the time this pass ever sees it (that rewrite is
# applied unconditionally to every block at construction time), so this
# pass recognizes instructions by the SAME hardcoded bm_op_hash constants
# interp.patlang's own dispatch chain uses, then EMITS ordinary,
# plain-STRING-tagged FuncIR instructions -- what self_hosting/lib/
# codegen_x64.patlang actually expects, since it knows nothing about
# this engine's own hashing scheme. This pass does double duty:
# de-hashing AND flattening/renaming, in one walk.
include "block_ir.patlang"

make a function called bm_nc_unsupported takes what returns nothing
  contract_check("bm_native_codegen", "assert", "Phase 9's translation does not support: " + what, false)
  return false
end

# Phase 19 (Global*/Handler*): both GlobalGet/HandlerLookup reduce to the
# SAME assoc-list "get" walk (bm_rt_assoc_get's own logic, interp.patlang),
# and GlobalSet/HandlerRegister to the SAME "set" walk (bm_rt_assoc_set's
# own rebuild-via-list_push logic, Phase 13's own fix -- never list_set,
# to avoid re-triggering issue #145's native x64 aliasing bug). Rather
# than bundling interp.patlang's own PatLang source into the native build
# (a real, separate option, not chosen here), these are hand-emitted as
# real Jump/JumpIfFalse loops directly, calling rt_list_get/rt_list_len/
# rt_str_eq/rt_list_push -- all four already confirmed present in
# self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.funcs, no new runtime primitives needed.
#
# Loop-internal jump targets use __RawJump/__RawJumpIfFalse (see
# bm_to_real_funcir's own header), backpatched via the identical
# "push a placeholder, record its own position, list_set it once the
# real target is known" technique bm_lower_stmt's own If/Match cases
# already use -- just operating on `out` (this instruction's own
# expansion) instead of a whole block's instrs.
#
# `uniq` (the ORIGINAL block-model instruction's own index within its
# block, passed in by the caller) plus `block_name`'s own existing
# prefixing keeps every synthesized local name globally unique, the same
# guarantee Load/Store's own bm_nc_prefix already provides for ordinary
# names.
make a function called bm_nc_local takes block_name, uniq, suffix returns out
  return bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__ah" + uniq + "_" + suffix)
end

# Pops (handler, name) already on the stack (handler on top, matching
# HandlerLookup/GlobalGet's own established pop order), pushes the
# looked-up value or false. `out` is the instruction stream so far;
# returns the updated stream with this whole loop appended.
make a function called bm_nc_emit_assoc_get takes out, block_name, uniq returns out2
  let handler_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "h")
  let name_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "k")
  let i_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "i")
  let n_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "n")
  let entry_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "e")
  let result_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "r")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_len", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", n_l])
  let loop_start = list_len(out)
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", n_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "<"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_notfound_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_str_eq", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_continue_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", result_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_done_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let continue_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_continue_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", continue_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "+"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", loop_start])
  let notfound_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_notfound_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", notfound_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "false"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", result_l])
  let done_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_done_pos, ["__RawJump", done_target])
  return list_push(out, ["Load", result_l])
end

# Pops (handler, name, ref) already on the stack (handler on top, matching
# HandlerRegister/GlobalSet's own established pop order), pushes a real,
# rebuilt updated list (never mutates the original -- same list_push-only
# rule as bm_rt_assoc_set's own interpreted logic).
make a function called bm_nc_emit_assoc_set takes out, block_name, uniq returns out2
  let handler_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "h")
  let name_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "k")
  let ref_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "v")
  let i_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "i")
  let n_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "n")
  let entry_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "e")
  let out_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "o")
  let replaced_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "rp")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ref_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_len", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", n_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 0])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "false"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", replaced_l])
  let loop_start = list_len(out)
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", n_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "<"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_loopend_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_str_eq", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_notmatch_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  # matched: push the UPDATED [name, ref] entry instead of the original.
  # rt_list_push(l, v) -- list FIRST, value SECOND (a real bug, found
  # here: this used to push the [name,ref] entry before the list,
  # backwards, corrupting the list via rt_list_push's own address/length
  # read against the wrong operand -- confirmed via a real segfault, not
  # assumed correct from the shape alone).
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ref_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_push", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "true"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", replaced_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_afterentry_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let notmatch_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_notmatch_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", notmatch_target])
  # no match: carry the original entry over unchanged. Same fix: list
  # (out_l) pushed first, value (entry_l) second.
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_push", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let afterentry_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_afterentry_pos, ["__RawJump", afterentry_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "+"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", loop_start])
  let loopend_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_loopend_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", loopend_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", replaced_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_append_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_done_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let append_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_append_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", append_target])
  # Same fix again: list (out_l) pushed first, the newly-built [name,
  # ref] entry second.
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ref_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_push", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let done_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_done_pos, ["__RawJump", done_target])
  return list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
end

make a function called bm_nc_prefix takes block_name, name returns out
  return block_name + "__" + name
end

# Phase 19 (Box*): pops (value, ptr) already on the stack (value on top,
# matching BoxSet/BoxSetUnchecked's own established pop order in
# interp.patlang -- popped_v = bm_st_pop(stack) happens FIRST, i.e. value
# is on top). Checked (unchecked == false): branches on
# bm_rc_touch_for_mutation(ptr) exactly like interp.patlang's own BoxSet
# -- refcount 1 mutates ptr's own cell in place and pushes ptr back;
# anything higher allocates a fresh cell, writes the new value there
# instead, and pushes THAT pointer -- leaving the original untouched, the
# same "reassign to the result" contract BoxSet already has interpreted
# (the caller must re-Store). Unchecked (unchecked == true): always
# mutates in place, skipping the refcount check entirely -- only ever
# emitted by lower.patlang's own flight check at a call site already
# proven statically safe (see interp.patlang's own BoxSetUnchecked
# header); matches its own identical pop order.
#
# bm_note_runtime_check_performed() (interp.patlang's own diagnostic
# counter, incremented on every checked BoxSet) is deliberately NOT
# replicated here -- confirmed by checking its only two call sites
# (self_hosting/block_model/spec_fixtures/flight_check_*.patlang) are
# both interpreter-driven (bi_run) fixtures, never read back by any
# native fixture or native_codegen_check.sh scenario, so it isn't part of
# Box*'s own observable native behavior -- a disclosed scope decision,
# not an oversight.
make a function called bm_nc_emit_box_set takes out, block_name, uniq, unchecked returns out2
  let v_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "v")
  let ptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "p")
  let discard_l = bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", v_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ptr_l])
  if unchecked then
    let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Store", discard_l])
    return list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  end
  let newptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "np")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_rc_touch_for_mutation", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "=="])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_clone_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", discard_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_done_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let clone_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_clone_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", clone_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "8"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_alloc", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", newptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", newptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", discard_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", newptr_l])
  let done_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_done_pos, ["__RawJump", done_target])
  return out
end

# Pass 1: translate ONE block's own instrs independently. Load/Store
# names get prefixed; Jump/JumpIfFalse targets are left as block-LOCAL
# indices for now (fixed up to absolute indices once every block's own
# start offset in the flattened array is known -- pass 2, below).
# JumpBlock is expanded here directly into Store (reverse param order,
# matching what's already on the stack) + a placeholder Jump, later
# resolved to the target block's own absolute start position.
make a function called bm_nc_translate_block_instrs takes block_name, instrs, print_op returns out
  # Returns [translated_instrs, index_map] -- index_map[k] is the
  # position in translated_instrs where ORIGINAL instrs[k] starts, for
  # k in 0..n (index_map[n] is the "one past the end" sentinel, for a
  # JumpIfFalse/Jump target that means "fall off the end of this
  # block"). Needed because JumpBlock expands into SEVERAL instructions
  # (a Store per forwarded value, plus a Jump) -- a JumpIfFalse/Jump
  # target computed against the ORIGINAL, one-JumpBlock-is-one-
  # instruction numbering does NOT equal its own position in the
  # EXPANDED output once anything earlier in the block was a JumpBlock,
  # which is exactly what a loop head (section 4.1) always has: found
  # as a genuine, reproduced infinite-loop bug (a tiny 3-iteration test
  # case hung indefinitely; self_hosting/block_model/bench/
  # diag_dump_funcir.patlang's own instruction dump showed the loop's
  # own JumpIfFalse landing MID-WAY through the back-edge's own Store
  # sequence instead of at the exit block's own), not assumed or
  # designed around in advance.
  let n = list_len(instrs)
  let out = []
  let index_map = []
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let index_map = list_push(index_map, list_len(out))
    let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
    let op = list_get(instr, 0)
    if op == 487138 then # "Const"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", list_get(instr, 1), list_get(instr, 2)])
    elif op == 277141 then # "Load"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 642543 then # "Store"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 451867 then # "Bin"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", bm_nc_bin_op_string(list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 862809 then # "Un"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Un", bm_nc_un_op_string(list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 34716 then # "JumpIfFalse"
      let out = list_push(out, ["JumpIfFalse", list_get(instr, 1)])
    elif op == 212209 then # "Jump"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Jump", list_get(instr, 1)])
    elif op == 6264 then # "Print"
      # print_op lets a caller pick which of two DIFFERENT, backend-
      # specific conventions to emit -- found to differ by checking each
      # backend directly, not assumed to be uniform: self_hosting/lib/
      # codegen_x64.patlang rejects CallHost "print" outright ("not a
      # real function and not in the OS-boundary allowlist") and needs
      # an ordinary Call to x64_runtime.patlang's own PatLang-defined
      # print function instead; self_hosting/lib/codegen.patlang (the
      # Rust-source emitter, used for the wasm32-wasip1 path) treats
      # "print" as a genuine host primitive instead (host_chunk_of maps
      # it to the "io_misc" chunk), matching CallHost -- the exact
      # opposite convention. tools/build_native.patlang passes "Call"
      # (the x64 path, unchanged); a Rust-backend driver passes
      # "CallHost".
      let out = list_push(out, [print_op, "print", 1])
    elif op == 974555 then # "JumpBlock"
      let target_name = list_get(instr, 1)
      let param_names = list_get(instr, 2)
      let pn = list_len(param_names)
      let j = pn - 1
      while j >= 0 do
        let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(target_name, list_get(param_names, j))])
        let j = j - 1
      end
      let out = list_push(out, ["__JumpBlockTarget", target_name])
    elif op == 296907 then # "ContractFail"
      # Phase 19: unlike this file's OTHER new opcodes below, ContractFail
      # carries no runtime-computed stack args at all -- its message is a
      # compile-time string baked into the instruction itself. Emits the
      # real 4-arg contract_check(func_name, kind, text, ok) CallHost
      # convention lower.patlang itself already uses for Assert (checked
      # directly, not guessed) -- func_name/kind are generic placeholders
      # since ContractFail's own single string already IS the full,
      # already-formed message (see lower.patlang's own "kind + ' failed:
      # ' + text" construction for Assert, and its own bespoke messages
      # for match/mut-check violations) -- nothing to reconstruct.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", "bm_native"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", "assert"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", list_get(instr, 1)])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "false"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "contract_check", 4])
      # contract_check DOES return a value under this real convention
      # (unlike this engine's own void ContractFail instruction, which
      # never pushes one) -- discarded here so it never lingers on the
      # native stack corrupting whatever follows. Same class of gap Fact
      # has below; caught building this same fixture, fixed at both
      # sites once found rather than only where it happened to surface.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
    elif op == 876321 then # "FiberYield"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "fiber_yield", 1])
    elif op == 46355 then # "Fact"
      # fact(pred, a, b) -- confirmed a real, already-compiled
      # x64_runtime.patlang function (line 6738), present in
      # self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.funcs (checked directly, not
      # assumed). Block-model's own Fact instruction already pushes its
      # 3 args in the exact written order fact(...) expects (pred, a, b),
      # matching lower_expr's own "push args in written order, argc of
      # them" Call convention exactly -- no restructuring needed at all.
      # This engine's own Fact instruction is deliberately VOID (never
      # pushes a result, matching Print) -- but an ordinary real Call
      # always leaves ONE, so it's discarded here to keep native
      # behavior matching the interpreter's, not left to corrupt
      # whatever this block does next (a real bug, found via this exact
      # fixture producing garbage output before this fix).
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "fact", 3])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
    elif op == 295774 then # "Query"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "query", 3])
    elif op == 289875 then # "TypeOf"
      # type_of(v) -- confirmed a real, already-compiled x64_runtime
      # .patlang function (line 6305), NOT missing as this plan document
      # originally (and wrongly) claimed -- found by searching for its
      # actual `make a function called type_of` definition, not by
      # grepping for a quoted host-call-name string, which is what
      # produced the false negative in the first place.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "type_of", 1])
    elif op == 229241 then # "ReadFile"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "read_file", 1])
    elif op == 36537 then # "WriteFile"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "write_file", 2])
    elif op == 54830 then # "HandlerNew"
      # An empty handler is just an empty list -- BuildList 0 is already
      # a real, working codegen_x64.patlang instruction (confirmed by
      # this file's own init_instrs use of it for the entry block's
      # __globals), no loop needed at all.
      let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 0])
    elif op == 841425 then # "HandlerLookup"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_get(out, block_name, i)
    elif op == 40752 then # "HandlerRegister"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_set(out, block_name, i)
    elif op == 695659 then # "GlobalGet"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_get(out, block_name, i)
    elif op == 708727 then # "GlobalSet"
      # Unlike HandlerRegister, GlobalSet writes the updated list
      # directly into "__globals" rather than leaving it on the stack --
      # matching interp.patlang's own GlobalSet semantics exactly (see
      # its own header note on why this one instruction uniquely writes
      # a local directly).
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_set(out, block_name, i)
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
    elif op == 258931 then # "BoxNew"
      # Pops one value (already on top of the native stack, pushed by
      # whatever instruction computed it), allocates an 8-byte cell via
      # heap.patlang's own bm_alloc (heap_chunk.obj, linked as a THIRD
      # extern chunk -- see tools/build_heap_chunk.patlang), writes the
      # value into it, and pushes the resulting pointer -- matching
      # interp.patlang's own BoxNew exactly (bm_ensure_heap_ready is a
      # no-op in this native path: the heap is a single flat bump/
      # freelist region backed by rt_heap_alloc, initialized the first
      # time bm_alloc actually needs fresh memory -- see bm_heap_init's
      # own lazy-init call inside bm_freelist_pop/rt_heap_alloc's own
      # first-touch path, not a separate step native code must call).
      let v_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "v")
      let ptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "p")
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", v_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "8"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_alloc", 1])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
    elif op == 251305 then # "BoxGet"
      # Pops ptr (already on top of the native stack) and pushes
      # bm_box_read(ptr) directly -- no Store/Load needed at all, exactly
      # like FiberYield/Fact's own single-Call translation above: the
      # value is already sitting in the right place for Call's own
      # pop-arg-then-push-result convention to consume it.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_read", 1])
    elif op == 264373 then # "BoxSet"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_box_set(out, block_name, i, false)
    elif op == 902425 then # "BoxSetUnchecked"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_box_set(out, block_name, i, true)
    elif op == 992319 then # "BoxShare"
      # Pops ptr, increments its refcount via bm_rc_inc (return value
      # discarded -- BoxShare is void beyond the refcount side effect),
      # then pushes the SAME ptr back unchanged, matching interp.patlang's
      # own BoxShare exactly. ptr is needed both as bm_rc_inc's own arg
      # and as this instruction's own result, so it's Stored once and
      # Loaded twice (an ordinary Call consumes/pops its arg off the
      # stack, so the first Load is consumed by the call and a second
      # Load is needed to leave the pointer behind afterward).
      let ptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "p")
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_rc_inc", 1])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
    else
      bm_nc_unsupported("instruction hash '" + op + "'")
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  let index_map = list_push(index_map, list_len(out))
  return [out, index_map]
end

# Bin/Un operator hashes (also bm_op_hash'd by lower.patlang's rewrite)
# need to become real operator STRINGS again for the real FuncIR's own
# Bin/Un instructions, which self_hosting/lib/codegen_x64.patlang parses
# as literal "+"/"-"/etc.
make a function called bm_nc_bin_op_string takes h returns s
  if h == 177616 then
    return "+"
  elif h == 177618 then
    return "-"
  elif h == 177615 then
    return "*"
  elif h == 177620 then
    return "/"
  elif h == 177610 then
    return "%"
  elif h == 861968 then
    return "=="
  elif h == 861044 then
    return "!="
  elif h == 177633 then
    return "<"
  elif h == 861935 then
    return "<="
  elif h == 177635 then
    return ">"
  elif h == 862001 then
    return ">="
  end
  bm_nc_unsupported("Bin operator hash '" + h + "'")
  return "+"
end

make a function called bm_nc_un_op_string takes h returns s
  if h == 499987 then
    return "not"
  elif h == 177618 then
    return "-"
  end
  bm_nc_unsupported("Un operator hash '" + h + "'")
  return "not"
end

make a function called bm_nc_assoc_get takes assoc, key returns v
  let n = list_len(assoc)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let entry = list_get(assoc, i)
    if list_get(entry, 0) == key then
      return list_get(entry, 1)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return false
end

# The whole translation: concatenate every block's own translated
# instrs, record each block's absolute start pc, then a final pass
# rewrites block-LOCAL Jump/JumpIfFalse targets to absolute indices and
# resolves each __JumpBlockTarget placeholder into a real, absolute
# Jump.
make a function called bm_to_real_funcir takes prog, print_op returns funcir
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let bn = list_len(blocks)

  # bi_run seeds the ENTRY block's own "__globals" specially
  # (bm_loc_set(bm_loc_new(), "__globals", []) in interp.patlang) --
  # nothing ever JumpBlocks INTO the entry block to populate it via an
  # ordinary Store, so without this init instruction, the entry block's
  # own prefixed "__globals" name would be read (via the forwarding
  # Load every block unconditionally has) before ever being written,
  # which codegen_x64.patlang's own undefined-variable check correctly
  # catches (confirmed directly: this exact error is what surfaced this
  # gap in the first place, not something anticipated in advance).
  let entry_name = bi_program_entry(prog)
  # Phase 19 (Box*): interp.patlang's own bm_ensure_heap_ready() lazily
  # calls bm_heap_init() exactly once, guarded by a get("__vars",
  # "bm_heap_ready") flag, before the FIRST BoxNew -- heap.patlang's own
  # free-list table (bm_freelist_table_addr's "__vars" entry) is
  # otherwise never set, and bm_alloc's own bm_freelist_pop ->
  # bm_freelist_slot_offset -> bm_freelist_table_addr chain reads it
  # unconditionally: skipping this init is a real, confirmed segfault
  # (peeking/poking through an uninitialized table address), not a
  # theoretical gap -- found by running this exact fixture before this
  # fix existed. The native path calls bm_heap_init() unconditionally
  # and eagerly at program start instead of lazily gating it on a flag:
  # simpler and equally correct (there is exactly one program start,
  # unlike bi_run's own single shared interpreter process that resets
  # "bm_heap_ready" to false per bi_run call), and cheap even for a
  # program that never touches a Box at all (one rt_heap_alloc(1024) for
  # the free-list table, nothing more).
  let init_instrs = [["BuildList", 0], ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(entry_name, "__globals")], ["Call", "bm_heap_init", 0], ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(entry_name, "__bm_discard")]]

  # Pass 1: translate each block independently; record where its own
  # (still block-local) instrs will start once concatenated -- offset by
  # init_instrs' own length, since those run first.
  let translated = []
  let starts = []
  let running_len = list_len(init_instrs)
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let blk = list_get(blocks, bi)
    let name = bi_block_name(blk)
    let result = bm_nc_translate_block_instrs(name, bi_block_instrs(blk), print_op)
    let t = list_get(result, 0)
    let index_map = list_get(result, 1)
    let translated = list_push(translated, [name, t, index_map])
    let starts = list_push(starts, [name, running_len])
    let running_len = running_len + list_len(t)
    let bi = bi + 1
  end

  # Pass 2: flatten, fixing up Jump/JumpIfFalse (block-local original
  # index -> its own EXPANDED position via that block's own index_map,
  # THEN absolute by adding this block's own start offset -- NOT
  # block-local original index + start offset directly, which silently
  # gives the wrong answer the moment anything earlier in the block was
  # a JumpBlock, since those expand into more than one instruction) and
  # __JumpBlockTarget (-> an ordinary, absolute Jump to the TARGET
  # block's own start).
  let flat = init_instrs
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let entry = list_get(translated, bi)
    let name = list_get(entry, 0)
    let t = list_get(entry, 1)
    let index_map = list_get(entry, 2)
    let base = bm_nc_assoc_get(starts, name)
    let tn = list_len(t)
    let ti = 0
    while ti < tn do
      let instr = list_get(t, ti)
      let op = list_get(instr, 0)
      if (op == "Jump") or (op == "JumpIfFalse") then
        let mapped = list_get(index_map, list_get(instr, 1))
        let flat = list_push(flat, [op, base + mapped])
      elif op == "__JumpBlockTarget" then
        let target_base = bm_nc_assoc_get(starts, list_get(instr, 1))
        let flat = list_push(flat, ["Jump", target_base])
      elif (op == "__RawJump") or (op == "__RawJumpIfFalse") then
        # Phase 19 (Global*/Handler* loop emission): a target SYNTHESIZED
        # entirely within bm_nc_translate_block_instrs's own emission of
        # one instruction's own expansion (an assoc-get/assoc-set loop),
        # not a real block-model Jump/JumpIfFalse at all -- its own
        # operand is ALREADY a valid index into THIS block's own
        # translated stream `t` (computed via list_len(out) as the loop
        # was built, backpatched the same way bm_lower_stmt's own If/
        # Match cases already do), so it needs only `+base`, never an
        # index_map lookup (which would misinterpret it as an ORIGINAL,
        # pre-expansion block-model instruction index and remap it to
        # the wrong position).
        let real_op = "Jump"
        if op == "__RawJumpIfFalse" then
          let real_op = "JumpIfFalse"
        end
        let flat = list_push(flat, [real_op, base + list_get(instr, 1)])
      else
        let flat = list_push(flat, instr)
      end
      let ti = ti + 1
    end
    let bi = bi + 1
  end

  let flat = list_push(flat, ["Const", "unit", ""])
  let flat = list_push(flat, ["Return"])
  return ["FuncIR", "main", [], flat, []]
end

# Namespacing note: an earlier version of this file bundled
# x64_runtime.patlang's own source directly into the same compilation
# unit as the translated block-model program, and salted (prefixed)
# every one of its ~326 function names to avoid collisions with a
# block-model program's own names. Tried, and abandoned after a REAL
# link failure: codegen_x64.patlang hardcodes raw `call
# rt_bigint_from_i64`-style asm text directly for overflow-checked
# arithmetic (x64_binop_asm_dynamic and similar), invisible to any
# IR-level Call/MakeClosure rename -- confirmed via that file's own
# header comment on emit_program_x64's `extern_names` parameter, which
# exists SPECIFICALLY because "most runtime calls... are hand-assembled
# string literals... invisible to any IR-level call-graph walk."
# Salting would have needed to also chase down every one of those
# hardcoded references, an open-ended, easy-to-miss list.
#
# The build now used instead (self_hosting/block_model/bench/
# build_phase9_native.patlang) sidesteps this category of problem
# entirely: a real two-CHUNK build, linking against the already-built,
# already-cached self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.obj (never re-lowered
# here) via emit_program_x64's own `extern_names` mechanism -- true
# linker-level namespace separation, which can't silently miss a
# hardcoded reference the way textual renaming could, since an
# unresolved symbol is a loud link error, not quiet misbehavior.
# Mirrors self_hosting/build_x64_runtime.patlang's own established
# two-chunk convention exactly, not a new invention.
